            <!-- Section 1 -->
            <section id="specialty-match">
                <h2>1. Match by Specialty, Not Reputation</h2>

                <p>The most costly mistake international patients make is choosing a hospital based on its <strong>general reputation</strong> — like picking a restaurant because it has nice photos online, not because it makes the specific dish you want well. In China, different hospitals have radically different strengths. A hospital ranked #1 nationally for cardiology may be mediocre at oncology.</p>

                <p>The right approach is a two-step process: <strong>first identify your exact medical condition, then find the hospital in China with the best documented outcomes for that specific condition.</strong></p>

                <div class="info-box">
                    <h4>&#x1F4A1; The Key Principle</h4>
                    <p>China's top hospitals are <strong>specialty-specific leaders</strong>, not general "best hospitals." Fu Wai Hospital leads in cardiology. West China Hospital leads in neurosurgery. Cancer Hospital CAMS leads in oncology. Each institution excels in its specific niche — and choosing based on general reputation can lead you to the wrong hospital for your actual condition.</p>
                </div>

                <h3>Why General Hospital Rankings Don't Help Much</h3>
                <p>Most "best hospitals in China" rankings are based on <strong>overall reputation scores</strong> — a hospital's name recognition, research output, and aggregate quality. These rankings tell you nothing about:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Which hospital has the best outcomes for <strong>your specific condition</strong></li>
                    <li>Which surgeon at those hospitals has the most experience with <strong>your exact procedure</strong></li>
                    <li>Which hospital has the best <strong>international patient services</strong> for your language and cultural needs</li>
                    <li>Which hospital publishes <strong>transparent outcome data</strong> for your procedure type</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Three Criteria That Actually Matter</h3>
                <p>For international patients choosing a hospital in China, focus on these three factors:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Specialty ranking</strong> — Is this hospital ranked in the top 5 nationally for your specific condition?</li>
                    <li><strong>Annual volume</strong> — How many of your specific procedures does this hospital perform per year?</li>
                    <li><strong>International experience</strong> — Does this hospital regularly treat international patients with your condition?</li>
                </ol>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 2 -->
            <section id="hospital-volume">
                <h2>2. Why Hospital Volume and Outcomes Matter</h2>

                <p>Research consistently shows that hospitals performing higher volumes of specific procedures have measurably better patient outcomes. This relationship — <strong>volume drives quality</strong> — is one of the most well-established findings in healthcare outcomes research.</p>

                <p>Chinese Tier 3 hospitals treat enormous patient populations due to China's 1.4 billion people. This creates surgical and clinical experience that is genuinely difficult to match in lower-volume healthcare systems.</p>

                <div class="info-box success">
                    <h4>&#x2705; The Volume Advantage Is Real</h4>
                    <p>A hospital that performs 5,000 cardiac surgeries per year will almost always have better outcomes than one performing 200 — even if both have excellent surgeons. This is because surgical teams, anesthesiologists, ICU staff, and post-operative care protocols all improve with repetition and practice.</p>
                </div>

                <h3>Volume Comparisons That Matter</h3>
                <table class="comparison-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Procedure Type</th><th>Typical Annual Volume (Top Chinese Hospitals)</th><th>US Hospital Average</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Cardiac bypass surgery (CABG)</td><td>3,000 - 10,000+</td><td>200 - 400</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Heart valve replacement</td><td>2,000 - 5,000</td><td>100 - 200</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Lung cancer surgery</td><td>2,000 - 8,000</td><td>150 - 300</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Liver transplantation</td><td>500 - 1,500</td><td>50 - 100</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>IVF cycles</td><td>10,000 - 30,000+</td><td>500 - 2,000</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Joint replacement</td><td>3,000 - 8,000</td><td>300 - 600</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h3>What to Ask About Outcomes</h3>
                <p>When researching hospitals, ask coordinators for this specific information — not vague claims of "excellence":</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Annual volume</strong> for your specific procedure (not "many surgeries" but an exact number)</li>
                    <li><strong>30-day mortality rate</strong> for your procedure type</li>
                    <li><strong>Average length of stay</strong> for your specific surgery or treatment</li>
                    <li><strong>Infection rates</strong> (surgical site infection rates are a standard quality indicator)</li>
                    <li><strong>Surgeon experience</strong> — how many of this specific procedure has your assigned surgeon performed?</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h4>&#x26A0; Red Flag Responses</h4>
                    <p>Be wary of hospitals that cannot provide specific outcome data, that give vague answers like "we're one of the best," or that claim 100% success rates. Serious, high-volume hospitals track and publish their outcomes and are comfortable discussing them.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 3 -->
            <section id="china-top-hospitals">
                <h2>3. China's Top Hospitals by Specialty</h2>

                <p>Based on the Fudan Hospital Rankings (China's most respected hospital evaluation system, covering 43 clinical specialties) and international patient feedback, here are China's leading hospitals by specialty:</p>

                <div class="hospital-grid">
                    <div class="hospital-card">
                        <span class="rank">#1 Cardiology</span>
                        <h4>Beijing Fuwai Hospital</h4>
                        <p class="specialty">Cardiovascular Disease & Surgery</p>
                        <p class="location">&#x1F4CD; Beijing | JCI Accredited</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="hospital-card">
                        <span class="rank">#1 Cardiac Surgery</span>
                        <h4>Beijing Anzhen Hospital</h4>
                        <p class="specialty">Coronary Artery Bypass, Valves</p>
                        <p class="location">&#x1F4CD; Beijing | High Volume</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="hospital-card">
                        <span class="rank">#1 Orthopedics</span>
                        <h4>Beijing Jishuitan Hospital</h4>
