            <!-- Section 1 -->
            <section id="visa-types">
                <h2>1. Visa Types for Medical Travel</h2>

                <p>China offers several visa categories relevant to medical tourists. The right choice depends on your nationality, the length of your planned stay, and whether you have a medical invitation from a recognized institution.</p>

                <div class="visa-card">
                    <span class="visa-type visa-type-medical">Recommended</span>
                    <h3>F Visa (Visit Visa) — for Medical Treatment</h3>
                    <p>The <strong>F visa</strong> is China's official visa category for non-commercial exchange visits, including medical treatment. It is the most appropriate visa for international patients seeking medical services in China.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Duration:</strong> Typically single-entry, valid for 3-6 months</li>
                        <li><strong>Stay permitted:</strong> Up to 90 days per entry (extendable inside China through the exit-entry bureau)</li>
                        <li><strong>Requirements:</strong> Official invitation letter from a Chinese medical institution</li>
                        <li><strong>Processing time:</strong> 4-10 business days at most Chinese embassies/consulates</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="visa-card">
                    <span class="visa-type visa-type-tourist">Also Available</span>
                    <h3>L Visa (Tourist Visa)</h3>
                    <p>For short medical visits (under 15 days for most nationalities), a tourist visa (L visa) may be sufficient, particularly if you have a simple outpatient procedure planned.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Duration:</strong> Single or double entry, valid for 3-6 months</li>
                        <li><strong>Stay permitted:</strong> Typically 15-30 days per entry (depending on your passport)</li>
                        <li><strong>Requirements:</strong> Hotel booking, itinerary, sometimes invitation letter</li>
                        <li><strong>Limitation:</strong> Not ideal for major procedures requiring longer recovery time; extensions in China are complicated</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box">
                    <h4>&#x1F4A1; Visa-Free Entry (2026)</h4>
                    <p>As of 2026, China has expanded its visa-free transit programs significantly. Citizens of many countries (including many European, Asian, and American passports) can enter China visa-free for short stays (typically 72-144 hours) via designated ports. However, <strong>this does not cover medical treatment</strong> — you still need an F or M visa for medical purposes, even if you can enter visa-free for tourism purposes initially.</p>
                </div>

                <table class="comparison-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Criteria</th><th>F Visa (Medical)</th><th>L Visa (Tourist)</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Medical treatment, medical exchange</td><td>Tourism, visiting friends</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Invitation required</td><td>Yes (hospital letter)</td><td>Sometimes</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Max stay per entry</td><td>Up to 90 days</td><td>Typically 15-30 days</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Extension in China</td><td>Possible via exit-entry bureau</td><td>Difficult</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Surgery, IVF, major treatments</td><td>Short consultations, minor procedures</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 2 -->
            <section id="visa-letter">
                <h2>2. The Visa Invitation Letter</h2>

                <p>The <strong>visa invitation letter (VOI)</strong> is the most critical document for obtaining your F visa. It is issued by the Chinese hospital's International Medical Center and is an official document addressed to the Chinese embassy/consulate.</p>

                <div class="info-box success">
                    <h4>&#x2705; We Coordinate Invitation Letters</h4>
                    <p>When you book through China Hospitals Guide, our coordinators at partner hospitals will issue the official invitation letter on your behalf. You do not need to arrange this separately — it's part of our coordination service. The letter typically takes 3-5 business days to process after you provide your passport details.</p>
                </div>

                <h3>What the Invitation Letter Contains</h3>
                <p>The official visa invitation letter for medical travel typically includes:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Your full name, nationality, date of birth, and passport number</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>Hospital name and address</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>Name of treating physician</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>Planned treatment/procedure</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>Planned dates of treatment and estimated length of stay</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>Hospital's official stamp and a contact person</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>Financial responsibility statement</strong> (confirming who pays for treatment)</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>How to Get the Invitation Letter</h3>
                <div class="step-box">
                    <div class="step-header">
                        <div class="step-num">1</div>
                        <div class="step-title">Confirm your hospital appointment</div>
                    </div>
                    <p>Once your appointment is booked with a partner hospital, the International Medical Center can issue the invitation letter.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step-box">
                    <div class="step-header">
                        <div class="step-num">2</div>
                        <div class="step-title">Submit passport details</div>
                    </div>
                    <p>Provide: full name (as in passport), nationality, passport number, date of birth, passport issue/expiry dates.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step-box">
                    <div class="step-header">
                        <div class="step-num">3</div>
                        <div class="step-title">Receive the invitation letter</div>
                    </div>
                    <p>The hospital sends the original invitation letter by courier (3-5 business days). Some hospitals can send PDF copies via email for faster processing — check with your coordinator.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step-box">
                    <div class="step-header">
                        <div class="step-num">4</div>
                        <div class="step-title">Submit visa application with the invitation letter</div>
                    </div>
                    <p>Include the invitation letter, your passport, completed visa application form, photo, and fee at your local Chinese embassy/consulate.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 3 -->
            <section id="visa-process">
                <h2>3. Visa Application Process</h2>

                <h3>Step-by-Step F Visa Application</h3>
                <div class="timeline">
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <h4>Step 1: Complete the online visa application form</h4>
                        <p>Go to the Chinese visa application website (cova.mvisaonline.com) and complete Form COVA. Print the confirmation page. Note: unlike the USESTA or Schengen visa, there is no fee calculation — simply complete the form and book an appointment.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <h4>Step 2: Gather your documents</h4>
                        <p>Passport (valid 6+ months), photo (48mm x 33mm, white background), visa confirmation page, invitation letter, proof of travel/accommodation.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <h4>Step 3: Book an appointment</h4>
                        <p>Book an appointment at your nearest Chinese embassy/consulate visa application center (CVASC). Processing times vary by location — check the specific consulate's website.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <h4>Step 4: Submit application and pay fee</h4>
                        <p>Attend your appointment, submit documents, provide biometrics (fingerprints). Pay the visa fee (varies by nationality and processing speed — typically $30-200 USD).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <h4>Step 5: Collect your passport with visa</h4>
                        <p>Standard processing: 4-10 business days. Rush processing available at most consulates for additional fee.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h4>&#x26A0; US Citizens: Note Special Requirements</h4>
                    <p>Chinese visa requirements for US citizens have changed recently. US passport holders may face additional documentation requirements. Check with your local Chinese embassy or use a visa service agency for the most current requirements before applying.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 4 -->
            <section id="travel">
                <h2>4. Travel Planning</h2>

                <h3>Major International Airports in China</h3>
                <p>Most international patients fly into one of these gateway cities with direct international connections:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Beijing Capital International Airport (PEK/PKX)</strong> — Best for patients going to Beijing hospitals; largest international hub in China</li>
                    <li><strong>Shanghai Pudong International Airport (PVG)</strong> — Best for Shanghai hospitals; major international hub with excellent connections</li>
                    <li><strong>Guangzhou Baiyun International Airport (CAN)</strong> — Best for Sun Yat-sen Cancer Center and other Guangdong hospitals</li>
                    <li><strong>Hong Kong International Airport (HKG)</strong> — Good alternative with easy connection to Shenzhen hospitals via train/bus; some nationalities benefit from Hong Kong's simpler visa transit rules</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Accommodation Near Partner Hospitals</h3>
                <p>Most major hospitals are located in central urban areas with extensive hotel options nearby. For international patients, we recommend:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Serviced apartments</strong> — Best for patients staying 1+ weeks; full kitchen, laundry, more space</li>
                    <li><strong>Business hotels</strong> — Western-chain hotels (Marriott, Hilton, Hyatt) near hospital districts offer reliable quality and English-speaking staff</li>
                    <li><strong>Boutique hotels</strong> — Often more affordable near major hospital areas</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Your coordinator can recommend accommodation options near your specific hospital.</p>

                <h3>Local Transportation in China</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Taxi/ride-hailing:</strong> Use <strong>Didi</strong> (China's Uber — works with international phone numbers) or regular taxis. Taxis in major cities are very affordable.</li>
                    <li><strong>Metro:</strong> Beijing, Shanghai, Guangzhou, and other cities have extensive metro systems. Use Google Maps or Amap (高德地图) for navigation.</li>
                    <li><strong>Train:</strong> For travel between cities, China's high-speed rail (G/D trains) is fast, comfortable, and affordable. Book via the 12306 app or at station ticket counters.</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="info-box">
                    <h4>&#x1F4A1; Payment Apps You Need</h4>
                    <p>China is largely a <strong>cashless society</strong>. You will need:</p>
                    <ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li><strong>WeChat Pay or Alipay</strong> — Linked to a Chinese bank account for most payments. International versions (WeChat Pay HK, Alipay+) work for some tourists but may not work at hospitals. Ask your coordinator about payment options.</li>
                        <li><strong>International credit cards</strong> — Accepted at most international hotels and some hospitals, but not universally.</li>
                        <li><strong>Cash (CNY)</strong> — Keep some cash for smaller hospitals and local purchases.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 5 -->
            <section id="checklist">
                <h2>5. Travel Documents Checklist</h2>

                <div class="checklist-box">
                    <h3>&#x1F9F9; Pre-Travel Documents Checklist</h3>
                    <div class="checklist-item">
                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Passport</strong> — Valid for 6+ months beyond your planned return date, at least 2 blank visa pages</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="checklist-item">
                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
                        <span class="check-text"><strong>F Visa (in passport)</strong> — Obtained from Chinese embassy/consulate with hospital invitation letter</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="checklist-item">
                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Visa invitation letter</strong> — Original or printed copy from the hospital</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="checklist-item">
                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Medical records in English AND Chinese</strong> — Translated and printed; imaging CDs if applicable</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="checklist-item">
                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Flight itinerary</strong> — Confirmation of round-trip flights in and out of China</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="checklist-item">
                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Hotel confirmation</strong> — Booking near the hospital for your entire stay</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="checklist-item">
                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Travel insurance policy</strong> — Including medical evacuation and medical coverage; print the policy document and emergency contact numbers</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="checklist-item">
                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Emergency contact information</strong> — Your coordinator's phone/WeChat, hospital address in Chinese characters</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="checklist-item">
                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
